<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<pretext>
    <article xml:id="worksheet">

<worksheet xml:id="wkst-26-card-disease">
  <title>The 26-Card Disease Model</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this activity, we model the spread of an infectious disease using a deck of playing cards.
      The model is intentionally simple, but it captures several important features of epidemics:
      randomness, transmission by contact, and the gradual buildup of immunity in a population.
    </p>
    <p>
      You will simulate several outbreaks and record how they evolve over time. Even though each group
      follows the same rules, the results may differ significantly.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <exercise xml:id="setup-26-card-disease">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Work in pairs or small groups. Each group will need one deck of playing cards (no jokers).
      </p>

      <p>
        Separate the deck into:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><p>26 red cards (susceptible individuals),</p></li>
        <li><p>26 black cards (immune individuals).</p></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Place all black cards face up in a pile. This is the <em>immune pile</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hold the 26 red cards face down in your hands. This is the <em>population pile</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Throughout the activity:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><p>red cards represent people who can still become infected,</p></li>
        <li><p>black cards represent people who are already immune.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="step-0-initial-infection">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <bold>Step 0: The initial infection</bold>
      </p>

      <p>
        Remove the top card from the population pile and place it face up on the table.
        This represents the first infected individual.
      </p>

      <p>
        Replace that card in the population pile with one black card from the immune pile.
      </p>

      <p>
        Record the number of new infections at Step <m>0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="step-1-transmission">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <bold>Step 1</bold>
      </p>

      <p>
        Shuffle the population pile without looking at the cards.
      </p>

      <p>
        Draw two cards from the population pile and place them face up on the table.
      </p>

      <p>
        Interpret the cards as follows:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><p>each red card represents a new infection,</p></li>
        <li><p>each black card represents contact with an immune individual.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Return any black cards to the population pile.
      </p>

      <p>
        For each red card drawn, replace one red card in the population pile with one black card from the immune pile.
      </p>

      <p>
        Record the number of new infections at Step <m>1</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="step-2-general-rule">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <bold>Step 2 and beyond</bold>
      </p>

      <p>
        Suppose Step <m>n</m> produces <m>k</m> new infections.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then Step <m>n+1</m> proceeds as follows:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>shuffle the population pile,</p></li>
        <li><p>draw <m>2k</m> cards from the population pile,</p></li>
        <li><p>count how many of those cards are red,</p></li>
        <li><p>replace each red card in the population pile with a black card from the immune pile,</p></li>
        <li><p>return any black cards drawn to the population pile.</p></li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Continue until a step produces no new infections.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="recording-data">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Record your epidemic in the table below.
      </p>

      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Step</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Number exposed</p></cell>
          <cell><p>New infections</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>0</p></cell>
          <cell><p>1</p></cell>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>1</p></cell>
          <cell><p>2</p></cell>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>2</p></cell>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>3</p></cell>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>4</p></cell>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
          <cell><p></p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        Continue the table as needed until the epidemic ends.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="graphing-the-epidemic">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Create a graph of your epidemic.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><p>horizontal axis: step number,</p></li>
        <li><p>vertical axis: number of new infections.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Compare your graph with those of other groups.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="initial-reflection">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Answer the following questions about your simulation.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>How many steps did your epidemic last?</p></li>
        <li><p>What was the largest number of new infections in a single step?</p></li>
        <li><p>Did the epidemic grow steadily, or did it fluctuate?</p></li>
        <li><p>At what point did the epidemic begin to shrink?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="class-comparison">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compare your results with other groups.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>Did all epidemics last the same number of steps?</p></li>
        <li><p>Did some epidemics die out quickly?</p></li>
        <li><p>Did some grow much larger than others?</p></li>
        <li><p>What features did most epidemics have in common?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="model-interpretation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Interpret the structure of the model.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>Why does the population pile always contain 26 cards?</p></li>
        <li><p>Why does the epidemic eventually end in this model?</p></li>
        <li><p>What real-world feature is represented by replacing red cards with black cards?</p></li>
        <li><p>In what ways is this model realistic?</p></li>
        <li><p>In what ways is it unrealistic?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="extensions">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Explore how changes to the model affect the epidemic.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>What changes if Step <m>0</m> begins with two infected individuals instead of one?</p></li>
        <li><p>What changes if each infected individual exposes three people instead of two?</p></li>
        <li><p>What changes if some individuals begin the activity already immune?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>


<worksheet xml:id="wkst-expected-value-epidemics">
  <title>From Simulation to a Mathematical Model of an Epidemic</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In the previous activity, you simulated the spread of a disease using a deck of cards.
      Each infected individual exposed two others, and some of those exposures produced new
      infections while others did not.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this worksheet, we develop a mathematical model that describes what typically
      happens in such an epidemic. Instead of tracking one specific outbreak, we study
      the <em>expected number</em> of new infections at each step.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <exercise xml:id="review-simulation-results">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall your epidemic simulation from the previous activity.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let <m>I_n</m> represent the number of new infections at Step <m>n</m>.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>What was the value of <m>I_0</m> in your simulation?</p></li>
        <li><p>What was the largest value of <m>I_n</m> that occurred?</p></li>
        <li><p>Did your sequence of values increase steadily, fluctuate, or decrease immediately?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="counting-exposures">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the card model, each infected individual exposed two other members of the population.
      </p>

      <p>
        Suppose there are <m>I_n</m> new infections at Step <m>n</m>.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            How many total exposures occur at Step <m>n+1</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If every exposure produced a new infection, what would <m>I_{n+1}</m> equal?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="introducing-probability">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In reality, not every exposure produces a new infection.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let <m>p</m> represent the probability that a single exposure produces a new infection.
      </p>

      <p>
        Suppose there are <m>2I_n</m> exposures at Step <m>n+1</m>.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            On average, how many of those exposures will produce infections?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Write a formula for the expected number of new infections at Step <m>n+1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="difference-equation-model">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The previous reasoning leads to the approximation
      </p>

      <me>
        I_{n+1} \approx 2p I_n.
      </me>

      <p>
        This is called a <em>difference equation</em>. It predicts how the number of infections
        changes from one step to the next.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>If <m>p = 0.25</m>, compute <m>I_1</m> when <m>I_0 = 1</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Use your answer to compute <m>I_2</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Continue one more step to compute <m>I_3</m>.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="growth-or-decay">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The model
      </p>

      <me>
        I_{n+1} = 2p I_n
      </me>

      <p>
        predicts whether an epidemic grows or shrinks.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>What happens if <m>2p &gt; 1</m>?</p></li>
        <li><p>What happens if <m>2p &lt; 1</m>?</p></li>
        <li><p>What happens if <m>2p = 1</m>?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="epidemic-threshold">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The condition
      </p>

      <me>
        2p = 1
      </me>

      <p>
        represents a boundary between epidemic growth and epidemic decline.
      </p>

      <p>
        Solve this equation for <m>p</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        This value of <m>p</m> is called a <em>threshold value</em>.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>What happens when <m>p</m> is larger than this value?</p></li>
        <li><p>What happens when <m>p</m> is smaller than this value?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="changing-contact-number">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the card model, each infected individual exposed two others.
      </p>

      <p>
        Suppose instead that each infected individual exposed <m>c</m> others.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Write a new difference equation for <m>I_{n+1}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What condition determines whether the epidemic grows?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="toward-a-reproduction-number">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The quantity
      </p>

      <me>
        R = cp
      </me>

      <p>
        represents the expected number of new infections caused by one infected individual.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>Explain what <m>R &gt; 1</m> means in this model.</p></li>
        <li><p>Explain what <m>R &lt; 1</m> means in this model.</p></li>
        <li><p>Why might public-health officials want to reduce the value of <m>R</m>?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="connecting-to-the-card-model">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Return to the original card simulation.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>Early in the simulation, was the fraction of red cards in the population pile large or small?</p></li>
        <li><p>Later in the simulation, did this fraction increase or decrease?</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            How did this change affect the number of new infections at each step?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Why does this explain why epidemics eventually end in the card model?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="preview-next-model">
    <statement>
      <p>
        So far, we have studied how the number of new infections changes from one step to the next.
      </p>

      <p>
        In the next activity, we will track three groups in a population simultaneously:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><p>susceptible individuals,</p></li>
        <li><p>infected individuals,</p></li>
        <li><p>recovered individuals.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        This leads to one of the most important models in mathematical epidemiology.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>

  <worksheet xml:id="worksheet-discrete-sir-model">
  <title>Building the SIR Model</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In previous activities, we modeled how infections spread step by step using a card simulation
      and a difference equation for the number of new infections. In this worksheet, we refine that
      approach by tracking three groups in a population simultaneously:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><p>susceptible individuals,</p></li>
      <li><p>infected individuals,</p></li>
      <li><p>recovered individuals.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      This leads to one of the most important models in mathematical epidemiology: the SIR model.
    </p>
  </introduction>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-variables">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose a disease spreads through a population of fixed size <m>N</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        At Step <m>n</m>, let:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><p><m>S_n</m> be the number of susceptible individuals,</p></li>
        <li><p><m>I_n</m> be the number of infected individuals,</p></li>
        <li><p><m>R_n</m> be the number of recovered individuals.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        What equation must always be true relating <m>S_n</m>, <m>I_n</m>, and <m>R_n</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-contact-assumption">
    <statement>
      <p>
        To model how infections occur, we make the following assumption:
      </p>

      <p>
        The number of new infections during Step <m>n</m> is proportional to both
        the number of susceptible individuals and the number of infected individuals.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Why should the number of new infections increase if <m>S_n</m> increases?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Why should the number of new infections increase if <m>I_n</m> increases?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Explain why it is reasonable to model new infections using a product
            involving <m>S_n</m> and <m>I_n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-infection-term">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose new infections occur at rate
      </p>

      <me>
        \beta S_n I_n,
      </me>

      <p>
        where <m>\beta</m> is a constant describing how easily the disease spreads.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            What does a larger value of <m>\beta</m> represent?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            What does a smaller value of <m>\beta</m> represent?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-recovery-term">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose infected individuals recover at rate
      </p>

      <me>
        \gamma I_n,
      </me>

      <p>
        where <m>\gamma</m> represents the fraction of infected individuals who recover
        during one step.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            What does a larger value of <m>\gamma</m> represent?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            What does a smaller value of <m>\gamma</m> represent?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-susceptible-equation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Susceptible individuals become infected, so the susceptible population decreases
        each step.
      </p>

      <p>
        Using the infection term <m>\beta S_n I_n</m>, write an equation for <m>S_{n+1}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-infected-equation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The infected population changes in two ways:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><p>it increases due to new infections,</p></li>
        <li><p>it decreases due to recoveries.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Using the infection term <m>\beta S_n I_n</m> and the recovery term <m>\gamma I_n</m>,
        write an equation for <m>I_{n+1}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-recovered-equation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recovered individuals come only from the infected population.
      </p>

      <p>
        Using the recovery term <m>\gamma I_n</m>, write an equation for <m>R_{n+1}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-full-system">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Combine your previous answers to write the complete system:
      </p>

      <me>
        S_{n+1} = \quad
      </me>

      <me>
        I_{n+1} = \quad
      </me>

      <me>
        R_{n+1} = \quad
      </me>

      <p>
        This system is called the discrete SIR model.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-conservation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the total population remains constant from one step to the next.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is, verify that
      </p>

      <me>
        S_{n+1} + I_{n+1} + R_{n+1} = S_n + I_n + R_n.
      </me>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-epidemic-condition">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose the disease is just beginning to spread, so that almost everyone
        is susceptible.
      </p>

      <p>
        That means we can approximate
      </p>

      <me>
        S_n \approx N.
      </me>

      <p>
        Use the equation for <m>I_{n+1}</m> to determine when the number of infected
        individuals increases from one step to the next.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your answer should produce a condition involving <m>\beta</m>, <m>\gamma</m>, and <m>N</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-basic-reproduction-number">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The quantity
      </p>

      <me>
        R_0 = \frac{\beta N}{\gamma}
      </me>

      <p>
        is called the <em>basic reproduction number</em>.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>What does <m>R_0 &gt; 1</m> mean?</p></li>
        <li><p>What does <m>R_0 &lt; 1</m> mean?</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            How does this relate to the threshold condition <m>cp &gt; 1</m>
            from the previous worksheet?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-preview-continuous-model">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The discrete SIR model tracks changes step by step. A closely related model
        instead tracks how these quantities change continuously over time.
      </p>

      <p>
        In that setting, the model becomes
      </p>

      <me>
        S'(t) = -\beta S(t) I(t)
      </me>

      <me>
        I'(t) = \beta S(t) I(t) - \gamma I(t)
      </me>

      <me>
        R'(t) = \gamma I(t).
      </me>

      <p>
        In later work, we will use this continuous model to predict epidemic peaks,
        vaccination thresholds, and long-term outcomes.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>

<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-compartment-models-parameters">
  <title>Compartment Models and Estimating Parameters from Data</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In earlier worksheets, we built the SIR model by tracking three groups:
      susceptible, infected, and recovered individuals. In this worksheet,
      we extend this idea to more general compartment models and learn how
      to estimate model parameters using epidemic data.
    </p>
  </introduction>



  <exercise xml:id="compartments-idea">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <em>compartment model</em> divides a population into groups that represent
        disease status.
      </p>

      <p>
        Examples include:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><p>susceptible individuals,</p></li>
        <li><p>infected individuals,</p></li>
        <li><p>recovered individuals,</p></li>
        <li><p>exposed individuals who are infected but not yet infectious.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Explain why it is useful to divide a population into compartments when
        modeling an epidemic.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="sir-review-system">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall the discrete SIR model:
      </p>

      <me>
        S_{n+1} = S_n - \beta S_n I_n
      </me>

      <me>
        I_{n+1} = I_n + \beta S_n I_n - \gamma I_n
      </me>

      <me>
        R_{n+1} = R_n + \gamma I_n
      </me>

      <p>
        Identify which term represents:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>new infections,</p></li>
        <li><p>recoveries.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="adding-exposed-compartment">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Some diseases include an incubation period during which individuals are
        infected but not yet infectious.
      </p>

      <p>
        To model this, we introduce a new compartment:
      </p>

      <p>
        <m>E_n</m> = number of exposed individuals.
      </p>

      <p>
        Individuals move through compartments in this order:
      </p>

      <p>
        susceptible → exposed → infected → recovered
      </p>

      <p>
        Explain why adding an exposed compartment may improve the realism
        of a model.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="seir-transitions">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose exposed individuals become infectious at rate
      </p>

      <me>
        \sigma E_n.
      </me>

      <p>
        Write equations describing how the exposed compartment changes from
        Step <m>n</m> to Step <m>n+1</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="seir-full-system">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Combine the infection term <m>\beta S_n I_n</m>, the progression term
        <m>\sigma E_n</m>, and the recovery term <m>\gamma I_n</m> to write a
        discrete SEIR model.
      </p>

      <me>
        S_{n+1} =
      </me>

      <me>
        E_{n+1} =
      </me>

      <me>
        I_{n+1} =
      </me>

      <me>
        R_{n+1} =
      </me>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="interpreting-parameters">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The parameters <m>\beta</m>, <m>\sigma</m>, and <m>\gamma</m> describe how
        quickly individuals move between compartments.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>What does <m>\beta</m> measure?</p></li>
        <li><p>What does <m>\sigma</m> measure?</p></li>
        <li><p>What does <m>\gamma</m> measure?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="early-epidemic-growth">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Early in an epidemic, most individuals are susceptible. This allows us
        to approximate
      </p>

      <me>
        S_n \approx N.
      </me>

      <p>
        Using this approximation, simplify the equation for <m>I_{n+1}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="estimating-growth-factor">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose infection counts during the first few days of an outbreak are:
      </p>

      <tabular>
        <row><cell><p>Day</p></cell><cell><p>Infected</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>0</p></cell><cell><p>5</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>1</p></cell><cell><p>8</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>2</p></cell><cell><p>13</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>3</p></cell><cell><p>21</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>

      <ol>
        <li><p>Compute the ratios <m>I_1/I_0</m>, <m>I_2/I_1</m>, and <m>I_3/I_2</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Estimate the growth factor of the epidemic.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="estimating-beta">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose the early-stage approximation
      </p>

      <me>
        I_{n+1} \approx (1 + \beta N - \gamma) I_n
      </me>

      <p>
        holds.
      </p>

      <p>
        Use your estimated growth factor to estimate the value of
      </p>

      <me>
        \beta N - \gamma.
      </me>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="estimating-r0">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall that the basic reproduction number is
      </p>

      <me>
        R_0 = \frac{\beta N}{\gamma}.
      </me>

      <p>
        Use your estimate from the previous exercise to approximate <m>R_0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="interpreting-r0-data">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose a disease has estimated value <m>R_0 = 2.4</m>.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><p>What does this number mean?</p></li>
        <li><p>Would you expect infections to increase or decrease initially?</p></li>
        <li><p>What kinds of interventions might reduce <m>R_0</m>?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="model-limitations">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compartment models simplify many aspects of disease spread.
      </p>

      <p>
        List at least three assumptions made by the SIR or SEIR models that may
        not hold in real populations.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>



  <exercise xml:id="model-extensions">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Realistic epidemic models often include additional compartments such as:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><p>vaccinated individuals,</p></li>
        <li><p>hospitalized individuals,</p></li>
        <li><p>asymptomatic individuals.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Describe one additional compartment you would add to improve realism,
        and explain how individuals would move into and out of it.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

